    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3189/002214308787780049",
    "abstract": "Abstract We propose a novel Eulerian algorithm to compute the changes of a glacier geometry for given mass balances. The surface of a glacier is obtained by solving a transport equation for the volume of fluid (VOF). The surface mass balance is taken into account by adding an interfacial term in the transport equation. An unstructured mesh with standard stabilized finite elements is used to solve the non-linear Stokes problem. The VOF function is computed on a structured grid with a high resolution. The algorithm is stable for Courant numbers larger than unity and conserves mass to a high accuracy. To demonstrate the potential of the algorithm, we apply it to reconstructed Late-glacial states of a small valley glacier, Vadret Muragl, in the Swiss Alps.",
    "field": "Glacier modeling",
    "problem": "Existing glacier models using moving meshes struggle to simulate surface evolution because adapting the mesh at each time step is computationally demanding and can cause problems and inaccuracies at complex ice margins with large curvature or steep slopes.",
    "method": "We propose a novel Eulerian algorithm to compute glacier geometry changes by solving a transport equation for the volume of fluid (VOF) on a structured grid, with the ice velocity field obtained from the full Stokes equations solved using finite elements on a separate unstructured mesh.",
    "task": "The task is to develop a novel Eulerian algorithm using the volume of fluid (VOF) method to compute the dynamic changes in a glacier's geometry over time for a given mass balance.",
